    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d687-a2-a1">

                <pb facs="1036" n="908" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg908"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">United States
                            Consulate</hi>,<lb/><placeName><hi rendition="#italic">La
                            Guayra</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1885-02-23"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 23,
                    1885</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Evan Jones</hi>,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Master of the brig Fleetwing, of Cardiff,
                    Wales;</hi></p>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Benjamin F. Cushman</hi>,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Master of schooner Addie M. Bird, of BocJcland,
                        Me.;</hi></p>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles G. Jackson</hi>,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Master Ship-Carpenter:</hi></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gentlemen</hi>: Application having been made to this
                    consulate by C. H. Cobb, master of the schooner Lanie Cobb, of Bangor, Me., of
                    the burden of 230.85 tons, or thereabouts, for survey upon said schooner in
                    consequence of having sustained damages and injuries on the 21st instant by
                    being run into by the Venezuelan schooner Ana Eulogia, whereby she is rendered
                    unseaworthy, you are hereby respectfully requested to repair alongside and on
                    board of said schooner, and after a careful and minute examination and survey of
                    every visible part of her hull, spars, sails, and rigging, report to this
                    consulate under your own hands in writing her present state and condition, and
                    what in your opinion had best be done for the interest of the parties
                    concerned.</p>
                <p>Given under my hand and the seal of this consulate the day and year above
                    written.</p>
                <table cols="2" rows="1">
                    <row>
                        <cell>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">seal.</hi>]</cell>
                        <cell>WINFIELD S. BIRD,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States
                                Consul.</hi></cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
            </div>
